From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: wevtsvc: the Event Log service keeps the logs

Wine's EventLog service (wevtsvc.dll in svchost, auto-start) did nothing,
and every event log function in advapi32 was a stub: ReportEvent printed its
strings to the debug log and nothing could ever be read back. Programs and
administrators have no Application, System or Security log.

The service now owns the logs. Every log named under
HKLM\System\CurrentControlSet\Services\EventLog is a file in
%SystemRoot%\System32\winevt\Logs (<log>.sgevt: a header, then the records
in ReadEventLogW's EVENTLOGRECORD layout, oldest first), loaded on first use
and created when a log is first opened. A log past its MaxSize loses its
oldest records (down to nine tenths, so the file is rewritten only now and
then); record numbers carry on after a clear, as on Windows since Vista.

Programs reach it over a private message-mode pipe, \\.\pipe\wine_eventlog,
one request per connection (include/wine/eventlog.h; advapi32 is the client,
the next patch). What a caller may do is decided from its own token
(ImpersonateNamedPipeClient, which 0140 makes real): everyone reads and
writes Application, System and other logs; only Administrators and SYSTEM
read the Security log and only SYSTEM writes it; clearing and backing up a
log is for Administrators and SYSTEM (ERROR_PRIVILEGE_NOT_HELD for a backup,
ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED otherwise). The pipe's DACL withholds
FILE_CREATE_PIPE_INSTANCE, so nobody else can serve the name.

The service writes 6005/6006 when it starts and stops and 104 ("The %1 log
file was cleared.", with the clearer's SID) to System, from the source
EventLog, whose EventMessageFile is wevtsvc.dll's new message table.
wine.inf creates the Application, System and Security logs (MaxSize 20 MB).

Gate: test/eventlog-gate.sh.
